In the context of phase selection, we must distinguish the following situations : out of equilibrium but stable phases and metastable phases at synthesis conditions.

- An _out of equilibrium_ phase lies on the convex hull of the system at synthesis conditions (temperature, pressure, ...) but is not present in the global equilibrium at the system composition. The system is thus out of global equilibrium but phases are locally in equilibrium.
- A _metastable_ phase lies above the convex hull at synthesis conditions, meaning that the system is out of both local and global equilibrium. Note that for this thesis, unless indicated otherwise, we only consider metastability at synthesis conditions, and do not consider phases that are stable at synthesis conditions and then quenched to room conditions when there are no longer stable.


